<!--
  AppViews: the window. What it shows and what a click does, in WPF.

  It is a library, and the app (../App) is the few lines that show it, for one reason:
  an agent cannot look at a window, but it can look at a picture file. Tests can draw
  what is in a library into pictures (Tests/AppViews.Tests), and cannot as easily draw
  what is in an .exe. spec/10-platforms.md, "Seeing what you have drawn".

  It may use the rules and the song. It does not read a file itself, and it holds no
  rule: what a click does to a song is worked out in Song, with no window in it, and
  shown here (AGENTS.md, "Keep the rules out of the window's code").

  What is where:

    LibraryModel    everything the window shows and does, with no drawing in it. In
                    four files: the main one, and what is about mending
                    (LibraryModel.Mending), about the old editor's copies
                    (LibraryModel.Folding) and about ChordPro files
                    (LibraryModel.ChordProFiles)
    LibraryWindow   the window, which frames LibraryView and tells the model what only
                    a window knows
    LibraryView     what the window holds: its menu, ListsPane at the left, SongPane at
                    the right, and over them the panels below while they are wanted
    ListsPane       the place and its libraries, over the songs of the library chosen
    SongPane        the song that is open: its name, the switch between its two
                    editors, its two keys, what is offered for it, and the editor shown
    SongLook        the song's header as far as it is numbers
    EditorSwitch    the two buttons, Chords and ChordPro

    ChordSheetView  the chord sheet: the strip of the key's chords over the sheet,
                    which scrolls
    ChordSheetCanvas
                    the sheet itself, drawn, and what the pointer and the keys do on it
    SheetGrid       where every slide, line and chord of the sheet stands, as numbers
    SheetMetrics    how wide words and chords' names are, asked of WPF
    SheetKeys       which key of the keyboard does what on the sheet
    SheetEntry      the bubble a chord is typed in, with what is offered under its box
    SheetLook       the sheet's colours and sizes

    ChordProText    the song as ChordPro text, to be typed in: WPF's text box, kept to
                    the song's words
    ChordProUnderlay
                    the same letters in their colours, drawn behind that box
    ChordProTyping  what the box works out with no box in it: the one change between
                    two texts, line breaks as the song counts them, the box's own undo

    QuestionPanel   what is asked before something is done that may not be meant:
                    Revert's question, and the two of putting a copy's chords back
    MendAllPanel    Mend All: its question, how far it has got, and what it did
    NewSongPanel    a new song from a ChordPro file: what was found in the file, and
                    what the song is to be named, look like, and have on a slide
    SongLines       a song's lines, to look at: an earlier version of a song
    EarlierVersionsPanel
                    the copies kept of the song that is open, and putting one back

    SettingsWindow  the settings, which frame SettingsView
    SettingsView    the settings' sections, each a tab
    ChordColourSettings
                    Chords: the colour of a chord in each of the two editors
    ColourPicker    the system's own picker of a colour
    MendingSettings Mending: its one switch
    AboutSettings   About: what the app says of itself, in AboutTheApp's words
    AllCopiesModel  Backups: every copy the app keeps, with no drawing in it
    AllCopiesView   that, shown: to look at a copy, or save it as a file

    ListsLook       the two lists as far as they are numbers and words
    ListIcons       the two small pictures that stand before names in the lists
    Look            the system's look, dark and light, and the app's own few colours

  Its tests (Tests/AppViews.Tests) may see what is kept inside it, for the few small
  things worth trying by themselves.
-->
<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0-windows</TargetFramework>
    <AssemblyName>Pro7ChordEditor.AppViews</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>Pro7ChordEditor.AppViews</RootNamespace>
    <UseWPF>true</UseWPF>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Pro7ChordEditor.AppViews.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\ChordRules\ChordRules.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Song\Song.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
